WEBVTT

opening
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.419
Can 4D foundation models remember? Introducing PersistBench.

observation
00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:15.318
Look at this person. Even after they leave our view, we remember where they were going towards.

question
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.110
Can a 4D model remember too?

brand
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.963
PersistBench benchmarks visual memory in 4D foundation models.

memory-question
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.212
How do we define visual memory?

memory-permanence
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:33.181
Inspired by cognitive science, we measure object permanence.

memory-motion
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.631
The second is motion continuity.

memory-appearance
00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.853
And the third is appearance preservation.

permanence
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:55.001
For object permanence, we track the object with SAM2 and verify its presence with a vision language model. We measure how often it is still there.

motion
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:09.700
For motion continuity, we compare predicted and reference mask centers, normalize their distance by object size, and turn position error into motion similarity.

appearance
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:26.397
For appearance preservation, DINOv2 extracts visual features. We average the features inside each object mask, then compare the prediction and reference representations.

data-gap
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.951
Without an out-of-view reference, how can we check the model’s memory?

another-view
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.423
What if another view could reveal the same moment?

360
00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.431
Our key idea is to use 360° video.

turn
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:50.124
As the event continues, we turn the camera and reveal the object again.

reference
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.582
This gives us real-world references at the same timestep.

evaluation
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.951
Now we can check the model against reality.

data-and-metrics
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:06.670
Our curated data and three metrics make visual memory measurable.

input
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.197
Give the model an input video and a target camera trajectory.

prediction
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.866
The model predicts the target view.

comparison
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:24.220
We compare prediction and reference at matched timesteps, then compute all three scores.

models
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:36.530
With two thousand paired sequences, we test twelve models across 4D reconstruction, camera-controllable video generation, and video-to-360° generation.

drop
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:45.420
Strong visible scores can collapse when objects leave view, with large drops across all three model families.

leaderboard
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:54.517
Some models remember more than others, but the gap between visible and out-of-view performance remains substantial.

closing
00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:01.573
How well does YOUR 4D foundation model remember?

explore
00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.393
Explore PersistBench.
